  "hints": [
    "Which node comes first in the result? Compare the two heads.",
    "A dummy head node removes the special case of an empty result list.",
    "Attach the smaller head to tail, advance that list, and repeat. When one list runs out, attach the rest of the other."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function mergeLists(a, b) {\n  const dummy = { val: 0, next: null }\n  let tail = dummy\n  while (a !== null && b !== null) {\n    if (a.val <= b.val) { tail.next = a; a = a.next }\n    else { tail.next = b; b = b.next }\n    tail = tail.next\n  }\n  tail.next = a !== null ? a : b\n  return dummy.next\n}\n",
    "py": "def merge_lists(a, b):\n    dummy = {\"val\": 0, \"next\": None}\n    tail = dummy\n    while a is not None and b is not None:\n        if a[\"val\"] <= b[\"val\"]:\n            tail[\"next\"], a = a, a[\"next\"]\n        else:\n            tail[\"next\"], b = b, b[\"next\"]\n        tail = tail[\"next\"]\n    tail[\"next\"] = a if a is not None else b\n    return dummy[\"next\"]\n",
    "explanation": "Walk both lists with a tail pointer, always linking the smaller head. A dummy node gives tail a starting point, so there is no special case for the first link. The remainder of the non-empty list is already sorted and is linked in one step."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n + m)",
    "space": "O(1)",
    "notes": "Iterative splicing avoids the O(n + m) call stack of the recursive version."
  },
  "followUps": [
    "Merge k sorted lists. Compare pairwise merging with a min-heap."
  ]
}
